# CHAPTER 1 · THE BASICS

Everything you need to survive — and then enjoy — daily editing:
modes, movement, the core edits, search, files, and the NvChad keys
of this config. Condensed from the classic vimtutor plus the NvChad
lesson pack. Work top to bottom; every shaded line is an exercise.

## 1.1 MODES

Vim is modal: the same keys mean different things per mode.

- NORMAL — commands. The home mode; you live here.
- INSERT — typing text. `i`{normal} enters, `<Esc>`{normal} leaves.
  In this config `jk`{normal} (typed fast, in insert mode) also
  leaves — your fingers never travel to Esc.
- VISUAL — selecting. `v`{normal} enters, `<Esc>`{normal} leaves.
- COMMAND — `:`{normal} opens the command line at the bottom. Your
  config also maps `;`{normal} to do the same.

Try the loop now: `i`{normal}, type a few words, `jk`{normal},
`u`{normal} to undo them. That undo key is your safety net for this
whole course: `u`{normal} undoes, `<C-r>`{normal} redoes.

## 1.2 MOVING

`h`{normal} `j`{normal} `k`{normal} `l`{normal} — left, down, up,
right. Use them for one or two steps only; for anything longer there
is a better key:

- `w`{normal} start of next word · `b`{normal} back a word ·
  `e`{normal} end of word
- `0`{normal} start of line · `^`{normal} first non-blank ·
  `$`{normal} end of line
- `gg`{normal} top of file · `G`{normal} bottom ·
  `42G`{normal} line 42
- Counts multiply ANY motion: `5j`{normal} `3w`{normal} `2b`{normal}

Playground — bounce around this block until the keys disappear:

alpha   bravo   charlie   delta   echo
one     two     three     four    five
left    right   up        down    stay

Drill: `gg`{normal}, then `G`{normal}, then back here with
`<C-o>`{normal} (jump back — more on that in chapter 7).

## 1.3 ENTERING INSERT MODE, PRECISELY

Six doors into insert mode — entering at the right spot saves all
the cursor shuffling:

`i`{normal} before cursor · `a`{normal} after cursor ·
`I`{normal} start of line · `A`{normal} end of line ·
`o`{normal} new line below · `O`{normal} new line above

ATTENTION: the ✓/✗ checks are EXACT — every character and every
space counts, and the sign updates the moment the line matches. If a
sign stays ✗, your line differs somewhere: press `u`{normal} until
the line is back to its original state and try again. (Autocomplete
popups are disabled inside the tutorial, so what you type is what
lands.)

EXERCISE 1: Complete the equation: press `A`{normal} (append at end
of line), type a space and then 4, press `<Esc>`{normal}. Target,
exactly: "2 + 2 = 4" (one space on each side of the = sign):

2 + 2 =

EXERCISE 2: Prefix the line below using `I`{normal} (insert at line
start), type NB! and a space, then `<Esc>`{normal}. Target: the line
starts with "NB! important:":

important: always read the manual

Practice `o`{normal} and `O`{normal} here — open lines below and
above, type something, undo with `u`{normal}.

## 1.4 DELETING

- `x`{normal} delete the character under the cursor
- `dw`{normal} delete from cursor to the next word
- `D`{normal} (or `d$`{normal}) delete to end of line
- `dd`{normal} delete the whole line · `3dd`{normal} three lines

EXERCISE 3: One letter too many. Get to the word with `02w`{normal},
step onto the extra p with `ll`{normal}, then `x`{normal}:

fix this typpo by deleting one letter

EXERCISE 4: Delete the duplicated word: `0ww`{normal} puts you on
the second "extra", then `dw`{normal}:

remove extra extra word here

EXERCISE 5: The sentence was typed twice in part. Navigate: `0`{normal}
then `4e`{normal} (end of "here"), `ll`{normal} (onto the space after
the period), then `D`{normal} wipes the rest:

this sentence ends here. ends here.

Line practice — put these two in the right order: `dd`{normal} on
the first one, then `p`{normal} (paste below the cursor line):

2) this line should come second
1) this line should come first

## 1.5 CHANGING

`c`{normal} is delete + enter insert mode in one key:

- `cw`{normal} change word · `cc`{normal} change whole line ·
  `C`{normal} change to end of line
- `r{char}`{normal} replace ONE character, no insert mode ·
  `R`{normal} overwrite mode

EXERCISE 6: `0w`{normal} onto the wrong word, `cw`{normal}, type
line, `<Esc>`{normal}:

this lime needs fixing

EXERCISE 7: Replace the entire line: `cc`{normal}, type exactly
fresh start, then `<Esc>`{normal}:

replace this entire line with something better

EXERCISE 8: One character is wrong. `$`{normal} to the end, then
`r9`{normal}:

press r9 on the zero: 0

## 1.6 COPY AND PASTE

- `yy`{normal} yank (copy) a line · `yw`{normal} yank a word
- `p`{normal} paste after the cursor/line · `P`{normal} before
- Deleted text is also "in the clipboard": `dd`{normal} +
  `p`{normal} moves a line (you just did it above)
- System clipboard: `<C-c>`{normal} in visual mode copies the
  selection OUT of nvim; in normal mode it copies the whole file.
  Paste FROM the system with `"+p`{normal}.

Practice: duplicate the line below a few times with `yy`{normal}
then `p`{normal} `p`{normal} `p`{normal}, undo it all with
`u`{normal}:

duplicate me

## 1.7 SELECTING: VISUAL MODE

- `v`{normal} select by character · `V`{normal} by line ·
  `<C-v>`{normal} by rectangle (block)
- Then act on it: `d`{normal} delete · `y`{normal} yank ·
  `c`{normal} change · `>`{normal} / `<`{normal} indent
- `gv`{normal} reselects the last selection

Try: `V`{normal} on the playground block in 1.2, `j`{normal}
`j`{normal} to extend, `>`{normal} to indent, `u`{normal} to undo.
Block mode gets its own chapter (8) — it replaces multi-cursor
plugins.

## 1.8 SEARCH AND REPLACE

- `/text`{normal} search forward · `?text`{normal} backward ·
  `n`{normal} next hit · `N`{normal} previous
- `<Esc>`{normal} clears the highlighting (your config maps it to
  `:noh`{vim})
- `:s/old/new/`{vim} replace first match in THIS line ·
  `:s/old/new/g`{vim} all matches in the line ·
  `:%s/old/new/g`{vim} whole file · add `c`{normal} to confirm each

Hunt: search with `/needle`{normal} and hop through every hit with
`n`{normal}: one is needle here, a needle there, and a last needle
further along — notice it wraps back around.

EXERCISE 9: With the cursor on the line below, run
`:s/mistakes/lessons/g`{vim} — the g gets both:

mistakes mistakes everywhere

## 1.9 FILES, BUFFERS, WINDOWS — SURVIVAL SET

- `:w`{vim} save · `:q`{vim} quit · `:wq`{vim} both ·
  `:q!`{vim} quit discarding changes
- Your shortcuts: `<leader>fs`{normal} save (leader = Space),
  `<leader>qq`{normal} quit everything without saving
- `:e path/to/file`{vim} open a file · every opened file stays
  loaded as a "buffer"
- `<Tab>`{normal} / `<S-Tab>`{normal} cycle buffers ·
  `<leader>x`{normal} close one
- Splits: `:vsp`{vim} / `:sp`{vim}, hop with `<C-h>`{normal}
  `<C-j>`{normal} `<C-k>`{normal} `<C-l>`{normal}, close with
  `<C-w>q`{normal}

## 1.10 GETTING UNSTUCK + HELP

- Stray into a weird state? `<Esc>`{normal} (maybe twice) returns to
  normal mode. Recording message at the bottom? `q`{normal} stops it.
- `:help anything`{vim} — e.g. `:help dd`{vim} · close with `:q`{vim}
- `K`{normal} on a word in this tutorial opens its help
- `<leader>fh`{normal} fuzzy-searches ALL help pages (Telescope)

## 1.11 NVCHAD QUICK TOUR — YOUR ACTUAL KEYS

Leader is `Space`. Press it and WAIT a moment: which-key pops up
showing everything that can follow. The full searchable list:

    :NvCheatsheet

- `<leader>ff`{normal} find files · `<leader>fw`{normal} or
  `<leader>fg`{normal} grep the project · `<leader>fb`{normal}
  buffers · `<leader>fo`{normal} recent files
- `<leader>e`{normal} file tree (you will need it less than you
  think)
- `<A-i>`{normal} floating terminal — `<Esc><Esc>`{normal} to get
  back out
- `<leader>th`{normal} themes · `<leader>n`{normal} line numbers ·
  `<leader>rn`{normal} relative numbers
- `<leader>/`{normal} toggle comment · `<leader>fm`{normal} format
  file
- In code files: `gd`{normal} go to definition, `K`{normal} docs —
  the full IDE workflow is chapter 9

## WORKOUT

Top to bottom, clear all nine ✗ marks without reading the
instructions, under 90 seconds. Then move on — the real fun starts
in chapter 2, where single keystrokes start replacing whole mouse
journeys.

